cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this code, please cite it as below and also cite the Panasonic 18650PF dataset."
title: "bmsoc: EKF / UKF / AEKF state-of-charge estimation with leave-one-profile-out benchmarking"
abstract: >-
  A reusable state-of-charge estimator package (coulomb counter, EKF, UKF, adaptive
  EKF) built on a temperature-aware second-order RC model with an explicit voltage-bias
  state. Benchmark on 24 Panasonic NCR18650PF driving profiles from -20 to 25 °C with
  leave-one-profile-out parameter calibration, then transferred unchanged to 24 CALCE
  A123 (LFP) discharge segments from -10 to 50 °C. Reference results: 2.9-3.8 percentage
  points SOC RMSE when the initial SOC is wrong by 30 pp; 1.7-3.1 pp for the adaptive
  EKF; 0.2-2.0 pp on LFP. Documented diagnosis of two UKF failure modes (inconsistent
  unscented weights and OCV clamped outside SOC in [0,1]). Calibration-margin probe
  shows capacity error (5-10 % -> 3-7 pp) dominates the error budget and cannot be
  absorbed by any filter state, whereas a ±40 mV OCV-table error costs 0.6 pp or less.
  Two stress tests bound the validity of these numbers: starting mid-plateau instead of
  full degrades initial-SOC error from 0.6-1.1 pp to 12-36 pp on LFP (a start-point
  effect, not a chemistry effect), and online capacity identification as a fifth filter
  state fails on real data because the 38-53 mV model residual is voltage-
  indistinguishable from capacity drift. The capacity error growth law
  err_end = -(1/s-1)(1-SOC_end) is verified point-for-point and generalises exactly to
  drifting capacity. An external SOH-anchor interface (SoHSchedule, piecewise-constant
  Q-hat(t)/R0-hat(t)) quantifies what a companion SOH estimator must deliver: bias
  within +/-2 %, staleness (walk rate x refresh period) within 4 %, and R0 within
  +/-25 % on NCA (R0 doubled costs +8.2 pp there, +1.4 pp on LFP). A fifth-round
  residual autopsy kills three of four candidate mechanisms (hysteresis worth 4.7 mV
  only, a third RC changes LOO residual by 0-1.9 %, slow residual is neither
  polarisation nor thermal) and identifies the true cause: OCV-curve shape error on
  LFP (strict-LOO Delta-OCV recalibration drops 37.4 -> 17.6 mV) and out-of-
  distribution cold high-power extrapolation on NCA (stays at 41.7 mV). A synthetic
  sweep sets the online-capacity-identification (EKFQ) feasibility threshold at slow
  residual <= 20 mV; below it EKFQ starting from a 5 % capacity over-estimate beats
  fixed-Q EKF (1.63 vs 2.44 pp), above it the v1.2 negative result stands. A sixth
  round separates two mechanisms behind the NCA residual: BV-type resistance
  saturation is real (HPPC onset resistance shrinks to 59 % at 10 A, -20 deg C) but
  static current-shaped corrections do not transfer; the dominant term is self-heating
  parameter mismatch (20 K cell swing, residual ~ 2.54 mV/(K*A) * dT * i, R2 0.66).
  Per-sample R(T_meas) correction cuts the us06 -20 deg C fold 181 -> 74.6 mV and the
  EKF median 3.21 -> 2.39 pp, while the cold low-current residual floor (42 mV) remains.
  Resolving the EKFQ boundary by residual structure revises the threshold to
  slow/even <= 20-30 mV and odd (current-correlated) <= 10-15 mV; an odd residual
  poisons the capacity state four times faster than slow drift. A seventh round
  dissects the cold low-current floor (59 mV at -20 deg C, |i| <= 1 A) by physical
  regime: 34 % of its low-current SSE is a constant-dv regime-mixing artifact of the
  open-loop metric (the start-of-profile rest reads e = dv constantly, and the table
  itself is self-consistent to 13.5 mV once rests are settled), 11 % is unconverged
  relaxation state at the end-of-profile 300 s rest (u_res +57 mV, slope +68 mV/300 s),
  and 55 % is a genuine dynamic model residual (45.9 mV) that is neither transferred
  by per-temperature bias/shape recalibration (LOO cut +1 %) nor a shared (u1,u2)
  kernel (cut -10 %) nor fixed by adding RC branches. Pre-registered verdicts: the
  OCV-table-inconsistency hypothesis is rejected as the floor cause, the dv regime
  inconsistency (142 mV at -20 deg C, cold-specific) plus unconverged state is
  partially confirmed, and open-loop floor is shown to be an upper bound on the
  filter-relevant residual. Tail extrapolation on 300 s rests requires a constrained
  tau prior (free-tau bias up to 26 mV; two-mode synthetic control 1.6 mV). An eighth
  round restates EKFQ feasibility in terms of the filtered residual (the innovation
  left after the EKF u/b states absorb what they can): states absorb 33 % of the
  open-loop floor at -20 deg C, the slow/even/constant content drops below 5 mV
  (so the slow-residual threshold premise is void in the filtered metric), and what
  remains is white / fast-nonlinear (29-64 mV at -20 deg C) plus an odd component
  over its 10-15 mV band in the cold. A selective-injection bench on a zero-residual
  synthetic plant identifies white as the sufficient main channel (white-only
  injection kills every fold, full replay reproduces the real failure exactly,
  zero-residual control converges 100 %) and odd as a cold-only secondary channel;
  the revised filtered-metric feasibility limits are white <= ~10 mV and
  odd <= ~5-10 mV. No NCA fold enters the feasible zone, so the negative EKFQ
  verdict on NCA stands with updated reasons (failure boundary lives in residual
  structure, not drive profile, DOD, or implementation). A ninth round identifies
  the white component physically: drive currents are ~0.5 A/s ramps (not steps) and
  the apparent dV/di on ramps reaches 150-300 mV/A = 2-2.4x the steady r0, so white
  is missing fast polarization dynamics (tau ~ 5-10 s) at current ramps/edges,
  amplified at cold temperature. Pre-registered verdicts: fast-polarization loss
  confirmed (ACF1 0.60, di/dt ratio 3.33, edge ratio 1.77, residualized fast-RC
  R2 0.38), charge/discharge hysteresis only marginal, measurement noise rejected
  (memory + di/dt concentration + heavy tails). The model-family exit is refined to
  a fast-polarization branch plus sampling alignment; filter-side robustification
  addresses the heavy-tailed innovation that poisons the capacity state.
